cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this paper or its Lean 4 verification artifact, please cite it as below."
title: "Formal Falsification of the Completeness of a Single Fixed-Memory Component for Open Inversion Tasks"
abstract: >-
  The paper proves, by a deductive impossibility argument, that no single fixed-memory component can be
  complete on its own for open inversion tasks: tasks whose observations are ill-posed, whose required
  context strength is heterogeneous and content-addressed by the evidence, whose inference inputs are not
  constrained by the training distribution, and whose demand rule cannot be given within any pre-given
  tolerance on the open set by any values frozen before inference begins. The argument runs along a
  decision axis: the content of a context rule is a set of numbers; values instantiated by current
  evidence or by analytic invariants are Mode A, and values frozen at inference time from training-corpus
  statistics or manual setting are Mode B. Generalization on the open set requires Mode A, while neural
  networks are Mode B carriers, so the falsification closes. The algebraic skeleton
  of the argument chain is machine verified with Lean 4 and Mathlib, with zero sorry and zero warnings,
  and the axiom budget of the load-bearing declarations is Lean's own standard set. This repository holds
  the JMLR LaTeX sources, the compiled paper, the online appendix carrying the artifact index, and the Lean 4 artifact.
